WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.400
It may not look like much, 

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.520
but that is one of the most important
archeological sites in Western Europe.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.640
When I say it's prehistoric,
I mean that literally.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.240
The ruins that are buried here
at Ness of Brodgar

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.200
are from around 5000 years ago,
when writing hadn't really been invented.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.920
And this... 

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.760
...this is an old worn out tire.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.800
And today, a team of volunteers
are putting 500 of these on top of that.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.920
- The Ness of Brodgar is a huge Neolithic
complex discovered in 2002

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.880
when we did geophysics across this area.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.000
Despite the size of the trenches,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
this only covers approximately
10% of the site. It's massive.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.520
It is a place of gathering,
5000 years ago.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.320
We can envisage hundreds
of people coming here.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.000
Neolithic society
wasn't a static society

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.480
and through the thousand
years that the Ness was in use,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.400
we see those changes
reflected in the architecture.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.440
What the Ness of Brodgar
has unlocked for archeologists

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.840
is a huge range of information.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.560
We have many new discoveries, new types
of discoveries at the Ness of Brodgar.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.200
The preservation is quite remarkable.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.160
Under nine inches of topsoil
we have Neolithic walls standing.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.080
And this is because this field has
never, ever been deeply plowed.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.120
- The reason for the tires, and the plastic
underneath, is a bit more obvious when

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.320
you know where we are:

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.360
the Orkney Islands,
off the northern tip of Scotland.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.360
The weather here can be brutal.
Sure, it's nice today.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:27.720
The temperature's usually mild. 

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.240
We're at the end of the Gulf Stream here,
so it rarely gets too hot or too cold.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.160
But we're next to the ocean.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.800
The wind is harsh and fast
and it blows in salt spray.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.760
The trouble with exposing the past
is that, well, you're exposing the past.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.600
- As soon as it's exposed to the air,

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.320
water can start getting into the very fine
laminations in the building stone.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.720
And literally, you'd see these walls start
to crumble at the end of each season.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.160
We cover the site with layers of plastic,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.200
then to hold all those covers in place,
all these hundreds of tires.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.760
I used to live at the top of the hill
just behind us, where we recorded once

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.720
a gust of over 120mph.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.520
And after some of the big storms
we have in the winter,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.400
despite our best efforts, 

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.280
sometimes you come back to site
and the corner of the polythene 

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.400
and some of the tires will have
actually been moved by the wind.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.360
- The old tires are a practical solution. 

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.760
They're free, they're heavy
but they can be moved fairly easily.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.920
And they're just soft enough
not to cause damage

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.640
when they're on top
of the plastic sheeting. 

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.400
They look ugly. They're going to disappoint 
any visitors for the next few months, 

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.600
but it means the site is
still going to be there, intact,

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.640
When spring comes
and archeology can start again.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:36.960
- We have to protect it.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.440
We have to do this
to the best of our abilities,

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.720
because these walls have stood
for over 5,000 years.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.400
And it's a top priority for us
to hopefully keep them there

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.560
and keep them in prime condition
for the next 5,000 years.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.360
- To be honest, they didn't
actually need the tire there.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.880
They just gave me one so
I could be out of the way of

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.840
the volunteers who are
doing the actual hard work.

